  "description": {
   "content": [
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "url": "https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/browse/FCREPO-2313#icft=FCREPO-2313"
       },
       "type": "inlineCard"
      },
      {
       "text": " addressed an issue where entity bodies were being included in responses that MUST NOT carry entity bodies, per RFC 2616.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "Unfortunately, the fix for ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "attrs": {
        "url": "https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/browse/FCREPO-2313#icft=FCREPO-2313"
       },
       "type": "inlineCard"
      },
      {
       "text": " was incomplete.  The WebApplicationExceptionMapper adds entity bodies to responses without regard for their status code.  In particular ContentExposingResource#evaluateRequestPreconditions(...)  may lead to an exception being thrown with a 304 response code.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "The consequences of this issue are the same as the consequences for FCREPO-2313- that is, application servers and clients get confused when processing responses that contain entity bodies when they otherwise shouldn't.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "In addition, the request logic is a bit unclear to me, but it seems that the intent is that if preconditions fail, an exception should be thrown by ContentExposingResource#evaluateRequestPreconditions(...). In that case shouldn't the response be a 412?",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    }
   ],
   "type": "doc",
   "version": 1
  },
